    "description": "Urban administration in Iran combines municipal service delivery, elected council oversight, and national regulation. Understanding a city's government requires distinguishing the municipality from the council and from the central government's territorial administration.",
    "tags": ["Local government", "Municipalities", "City councils", "Urban planning", "Public services"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:39:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:39:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Administration in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's municipalities, city councils, urban services, and the distinction between local responsibilities and national authority."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "constitutional-foundation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Local representation within a national framework",
        "content": "Iran's Constitution gives local councils a place in the administration of public affairs. Article 100 provides for councils elected by their local communities and connects their work to development, welfare, public health, culture, and education. However, it leaves their powers and electoral arrangements to legislation, explicitly preserving central-government sovereignty. Local representation therefore operates within a national legal framework rather than a constitutionally separate sphere of municipal sovereignty. [s1]\n\nArticle 103 requires centrally appointed territorial officials to respect council decisions made within the councils' jurisdiction. That qualification matters: a council decision must concern a subject over which the council actually has authority. The Constitution establishes a relationship between local representation and territorial administration; it does not make every public institution located in a city subordinate to its council. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "municipal-work",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The municipality as an operating institution",
        "content": "A municipality, called a shahrdari in Persian, has its own legal personality under the Municipality Law. The law's catalogue of responsibilities includes streets, public gardens, drainage channels, public-space maintenance, and urban cleanliness. These are tangible responsibilities: maintaining a public route or organizing waste disposal requires staff, equipment, land, and an operating budget. [s2]\n\nThe same legislation also addresses municipal property, transactions, and budget preparation. Municipal administration consequently involves both visible services and the management of public assets. A service announcement alone reveals little about its financing, procurement, or continuing maintenance. Those questions require examination of budgets and implementation records. [s2]\n\nHistorical legal wording needs care. The inspected compilation identifies repealed provisions and references earlier municipal councils and institutions. It should be read as a layered legal record, alongside subsequent legislation, rather than as an unchanged description of every present-day service arrangement. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "council-and-mayor",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Council oversight and executive responsibility",
        "content": "The councils legislation assigns city councils the selection of the mayor and approval of municipal budgets and accounts. It also gives them responsibilities for scrutinizing municipal assets and expenditure. The council and municipality are therefore distinct: one exercises representative oversight and specified approval powers, while the other administers municipal work. [s3]\n\nThis distinction helps explain accountability. A decision to authorize spending and the subsequent delivery of a service are different stages. Evaluating municipal government requires evidence about both, rather than treating approval as proof of completion. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coordination-and-reform",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where administrative boundaries become a problem",
        "content": "Urban management also depends on institutions beyond a municipality. A study of Tehran's metropolitan planning identified fragmentation across management, functions, political authority, and territorial jurisdictions. Its findings concern the arrangements examined in that research; they should not be read as a measured assessment of every Iranian city today. Nevertheless, the study illustrates why the existence of a municipality does not by itself establish integrated control over an urban region. [s4]\n\nThe National Urban Policy and Smart City Strategy summary published in 2022 identifies integrated management, sustainable municipal revenue, citizen participation, and metropolitan governance as policy priorities. These are proposed directions for institutional improvement, rather than evidence that reforms have been completed. [s5]\n\nThe branches below separate these questions into more manageable subjects: Tehran's municipal organization, metropolitan coordination, city and village councils, and the organizations that deliver urban services. The useful starting point is always the same: identify the territory, the responsible institution, and the evidence showing what it has actually done.",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
