    "description": "Iran's metropolitan municipalities administer major cities within urban regions that can extend beyond municipal boundaries. Their central governance challenge is coordinating local decisions with neighboring settlements and institutions responsible for regional development.",
    "tags": ["Metropolitan governance", "Municipalities", "Urban regions", "Spatial planning", "Intergovernmental coordination"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:39:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:39:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Metropolitan Municipalities in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand municipal boundaries, different patterns of metropolitan growth, and Iran's documented priorities for regional urban governance."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "municipality-and-region",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A large city and its wider urban region",
        "content": "A metropolitan municipality and a metropolitan region describe different things. The first is an institution administering a city; the second is a wider spatial system comprising a central urban area and surrounding settlements. Research on Tehran uses this broader regional perspective to examine why urban planning involves several authorities rather than one municipal executive. [s1]\n\nThe distinction changes how a governance problem should be understood. A municipality can exercise responsibilities inside its jurisdiction while development in neighboring settlements alters the wider region. Metropolitan coordination concerns the connections between those places, not simply the internal organization of the central city's government. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "different-spatial-patterns",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different cities produce different coordination needs",
        "content": "A comparative study published in 2018 examined Tehran, Mashhad, Isfahan, and Shiraz using measures of spatial concentration and distribution. Its abstract describes Tehran's development as increasingly polycentric, with dispersed growth in peripheral and rural areas. Polycentric means that several settlement centers contribute to the region's structure, even where a principal city remains dominant. [s2]\n\nThe study identified different configurations elsewhere: a radial pattern around Isfahan, a predominantly single-center linear pattern in Mashhad, and a dominant center with peripheral sprawl in Shiraz. These are findings from a particular study, not permanent classifications or verified descriptions of conditions in 2026. [s2]\n\nTheir explanatory value lies in showing why a uniform institutional template may miss important differences. Coordinating several expanding centers raises different spatial questions from managing development along a principal corridor. That is an interpretation of the documented patterns, rather than a claim that one city's government performs better than another. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fragmentation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why coordination is an institutional issue",
        "content": "The Tehran management study identifies fragmented authority and weak coordination across planning functions and territories. It also argues that national-government institutions dominate important aspects of spatial decision-making, limiting the position of municipalities and councils. These findings challenge the assumption that the central-city mayor controls the entire metropolitan region. They remain historical research findings, not a current audit of every relevant institution. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "policy-direction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recognizing the metropolitan scale",
        "content": "The 2022 National Urban Policy and Smart City Strategy summary explicitly calls for defining metropolitan areas within Iran's planning and governance system. It proposes more effective legal and institutional frameworks for metropolitan governance, alongside integrated urban management and sustainable municipal revenue. [s3]\n\nThe report is especially useful because it treats metropolitan governance as a distinct policy problem. Its recommendations establish a reform agenda; they do not establish that a new regional authority was created or that coordination problems were resolved. [s3]\n\nFor an international reader, the key question is therefore not merely how large a municipality is. It is whether the institutions responsible for connected settlements can plan and act together. Population totals, organizational titles, and announced projects each reveal only part of that relationship. The documented distinction between municipal and metropolitan scales provides a firmer starting point. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
